germany is struggling to cope with a  surge of covid19 infections  despite being in a soft lockdown for  five months the number of cases is going  up  and hospitals say they're being pushed  to the limit today the cabinet here in  berlin agreed to approve  new national measures to try to bring  down infection rates  these new measures require a change in  the law to allow what is being called  an emergency break to be imposed in all  parts of the country if certain  conditions are met  now this new law would oblige all  regions to implement this  emergency break if there are more than  100 cova cases  per 100 000 people in a seven day period  then  a curfew would be imposed between the  hours of 9 p.m  and 5 a.m and then all non-essential  shops  would also be closed  chancellor angela merkel welcomed what  she called the urgently needed decision  by the government in order to improve  the situation and to slow down the third  wave  to break it and turn it around the  measures implemented thus far by the  state and regions don't go far enough  our response to the pandemic must be  more stringent and consequential  consequently  let's bring in our chief political  editor michelle kufner who is standing  outside of the chancellery  chancellory for us mr ella you were  listening into the chancellor's message  tell us more about  what you had to say well she basically  called this step of now  implementing a national um drive a  national emergency break  as a long overdue as setting the corona  fight  on a new footing and what that means is  that angela marco  took germany's corona response next  level  instead of continuing to gather the 16  state premiers around her  getting an agreed paper and then  everybody going  into their federal state and doing  whatever they like pretty much  she's now making this legally binding  well she doesn't mean that she can do it  alone she still  needs that signature on the dotted line  by enough mps she's taking this  to parliament and she's getting the  states to agree to it and that would  then make it legally binding  and there would be an immediate  emergency break implemented at that  magic figure of 100 infections for 100  000 people within the space of seven  days and that already applies to pretty  much  most of the country why is this question  of taking a national approach so  controversial  because there was a lot of credibility  lost when state premiers didn't stick to  what they  said and kept carving out exceptions for  their own states that has created a lot  of confusion that has cost  everybody a lot of public trust who  works in public policy  and that is also to blame for angela  mackel's own party dipping  in the opinion polls and these latest  infection rates going up once again we  are in the midst of that third wave here  in germany  and a majority of germans is actually  for a tougher  lockdown and if you ask people on the  street they certainly want to know  which rules apply wherever they go  and not on a 100 kilometer drive have  three different sets of regulations  apply  you said that the chancellor will have  to take this new law to parliament what  kind of support will she have for it  there  well it looks like she is getting enough  support to  take it to parliament the big question  now is will it be fast track  can this all be decided this week the  parliamentary groups  are currently meeting as we speak here  so still  more intense debates to be had but this  looks like it's  definitely going ahead in germany with  experts warning hospitals warning that  it's simply  coming too late angela mackel herself  said  if we i said a long time ago she said if  we wait  until those intensive care beds are  filling up  and are full then it's too late and  germany is pretty much nearing that  point right now  our chief political editor michele  lacuna with the latest thank you  now aerosol researchers are criticizing  coronavirus measures and they are  calling for a radical rethink they say  infections  outside hardly take place and we can  speak now to christoph aspak he's the  president of the society for aerosol  research he's taken  part in this research thank you very  much for joining us  now tell us what the research shows  about how covid  is being transmitted what are the  findings here  well first of all we know we have known  for several months already that it's  mostly an aerosol transmission so  airborne transmission of the virus  um and at the same time we know that  aerosols or aerosol particles as we  exhale them they're quickly diluted when  you're outdoors because there are always  wind  currents there's always a thermal motion  upwards  and that quickly dilutes the virus  concentration and therefore it's very  unlikely that you  get infected outdoors whereas indoors  it's much more likely because the virus  concentration can  accumulate and there has been a recent  study published in ireland  and they checked more than 230 000  infections and they found that 99.9  of them actually occurred indoors and  that's  what we try to make sure that everyone  understands and also the general public  that  it's the most the highest infection risk  is actually indoors and not outdoors  so you're saying basically the measures  that are currently in place they don't  prevent infection they might even  be counterproductive so which measures  would that be that they don't help  really  well what we criticize is that there's  two  too large focus on measures for outdoors  whereas outdoors not nothing really or  almost no infections occur  these include the duty to wear a mask  when you're outdoors that several places  which are very popular are locked  like along the rhine river for example  here in in my area  and that doesn't really help because we  should focus a lot more on the indoor  infections and that applies  to well all indoor environments that  includes  private homes that includes workplaces  that includes shops that 